schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Quadratic funding formula and the breadth-over-wealth design goal
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    For each project, take the square root of every individual donation, sum
    those roots, then square the total — that squared number is the
    project's share of a shared matching pool. The design goal: the number of
    distinct backers matters more than the size of any single donation.
  source_traditions:
    - >-
      "Liberal Radicalism: A Flexible Design for Philanthropic Matching
      Funds," a 2018 paper by Vitalik Buterin, Zoë Hitzig, and Glen Weyl
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Walked through with a worked example in the article (100 people giving $1
    each outweighs one person giving $100, 10,000 vs. 100 matching weight)
    to make the "breadth beats whales" design goal concrete for organizers
    deciding whether to run a round.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - >-
      Deep Funding, MACI private voting, Conviction Voting, Retroactive
      Funding, and peer-reviewed Hypercerts — the five other mechanisms
      GG24 ran alongside QF in the same cycle, per the article's "plural
      toolkit" framing.
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      Democratic communities systematically underfund shared goods because
      no single person captures the full benefit — QF is designed to correct
      for that specific market failure, not to replace grants generally.
  tensions:
    - >-
      Rewarding breadth of support vs. vulnerability to sybil attacks (one
      person spinning up many wallets to fake a crowd).
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Reducing QF to "more donors equals more match" without the sybil-defense
    caveats (proof of personhood, cluster-matching corrections) that make a
    round's match trustworthy in practice.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/gitcoin-grants-qf.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
